  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist and are verified by reading them back from Kibana.\n\n**Data view (`kibana_sample_data_logs`)**\n- 14,074 requests from 1,001 unique client IPs, across 448 unique URLs.\n- `response` is a string with a `.keyword` subfield. Values are 200 (12,832), 404 (801), and 503 (441).\n- Other useful fields are `bytes`, `clientip`, `geo.dest` (CN, IN, US, ID, BR lead), `extension`, and `agent`.\n- The timestamps run from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03. Today is 2026-10-12, so part of the data is in the future.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-9263feb8 Web logs overview`** (id `85495daa-b036-46db-851c-a855888c75ea`)\n- Four metric panels: total requests, 5xx responses (KQL `response.keyword: 5*`), unique client IPs, and bytes served.\n- One stacked bar chart of requests over time, broken down by response code.\n- I set the dashboard's time range to 2026-10-04 through 2026-12-04 so the data is visible. The default \"last 7 days\" would show almost nothing.\n\n**Alert rule: `EFF-9263feb8 5xx spike`** (id `4f45d940-308e-46fd-adfc-bfce0b802e60`)\n- Type: Elasticsearch query (`.es-query`), KQL-based, on the web-logs data view.\n- Runs every 1 minute. Counts documents matching `response.keyword: 5*` over the last 5 minutes. Fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Enabled. Status is `ok` after the first run.\n\nThings to know:\n- **The rule is unlikely to fire on this data.** The 5xx rate is about 7 per day, roughly 0.02 per 5-minute window. The threshold of 10 will almost never be crossed. The rule is correct, but it won't show a spike unless new traffic arrives.\n- **No notification action is attached.** The rule will create alert instances in Kibana but send nothing. If you want email, Slack, or webhook notifications, I'll need a connector to attach.",
